                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 190,
                "text": "next digit 6 → round up",
                "size": 22,
                "color": "purple",
                "change": "highlight",
                "bold": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "6 is 5 or more, so round up. 48 becomes 49.",
          "write": "49 …"
        },
        {
          "say": "Keep the place value. The answer is 49 000, not 49.",
          "write": "48 615 ≈ 49 000"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Multiplying and dividing",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Numbers with numbers, powers with powers.",
          "write": "(6 × 10⁵) × (4 × 10⁻⁹)"
        },
        {
          "say": "6 times 4 is 24. Add the powers: 5 plus minus 9 is minus 4.",
          "write": "24 × 10⁻⁴"
        },
        {
          "say": "24 is too big for the front. Make it 2.4, and the power goes up by 1.",
          "write": "= 2.4 × 10⁻³",
          "draw": {
            "type": "shapes",
            "width": 560,
            "height": 230,
            "alt": "Tidy the front number",
            "items": [
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 80,
                "text": "24 × 10⁻⁴",
                "size": 36,
                "color": "black",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 180,
                "text": "2.4 × 10⁻³",
                "size": 36,
                "color": "purple",
                "change": "add",
                "bold": true
              },
              {
                "kind": "arrow",
                "x": 280,
                "y": 100,
                "x2": 280,
                "y2": 145,
                "color": "green",
                "change": "add"
              },
              {
                "kind": "text",
                "x": 440,
                "y": 130,
                "text": "÷10 front, +1 power",
                "size": 16,
                "color": "green",
                "change": "add",
                "bold": true
              }
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "say": "To divide, divide the numbers and subtract the powers.",
          "write": "(3.6 × 10⁴) ÷ (9 × 10⁻³)"
        },
        {
          "say": "3.6 over 9 is 0.4. 4 minus minus 3 is 7.",
          "write": "0.4 × 10⁷"
        },
        {
          "say": "0.4 is too small. Make it 4, and the power drops by 1.",
          "write": "= 4 × 10⁶"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "How precise should an answer be?",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "An answer can't be more precise than the data."
        },
        {
          "say": "Use the same significant figures as the least precise measurement.",
          "write": "match the least precise"
        },
        {
          "say": "Light travels at 3.00 times ten to the 8 metres per second.",
          "write": "3.00 × 10⁸ m/s"
        },
        {
          "say": "The Sun is 1.50 times ten to the 11 metres away. Time is distance over speed.",
          "write": "(1.50 × 10¹¹) ÷ (3.00 × 10⁸)"
        },
        {
          "say": "That's 0.5 times ten cubed, which is 5.00 times ten squared seconds.",
          "write": "5.00 × 10² s"
        },
        {
          "say": "That's 500 seconds, a bit over 8 minutes.",
          "write": "≈ 8 minutes"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Watch out for these",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "The front number must be between 1 and 10.",
          "write": "✗ 24 × 10⁻⁴"
        },
        {
          "say": "Leading zeros aren't significant. 0.0305 has 3 significant figures.",
          "write": "0.0305: 3 s.f."
        },
        {
          "say": "When multiplying, add the powers. Don't multiply them.",
          "write": "10⁶ × 10⁻² = 10⁴"
        },
        {
          "say": "Estimate first. Should the answer be huge or tiny?",
          "write": "estimate first"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "heading": "Remember",
      "lines": [
        {
          "say": "Big is positive, small is negative.",
          "write": "big → +,  small → −"
        },
        {
          "say": "Start at the first non-zero digit.",
          "write": "first non-zero"
        },
        {
          "say": "Numbers with numbers, powers with powers. Then tidy up.",
          "write": "1 ≤ a < 10"
        },
        {
          "say": "Match the least precise measurement.",
          "write": "same s.f. as the data"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}